##  {background-image="assets/content-bg.png" background-size="cover"}

[CHAPTER ONE]{.section-label}
[The question every student asks]{.slide-title}

::: {.spread}

[“Why am I learning this?”]{.big-quote}

::: {.body-text .narrow-text}
I am going to answer that today, with a story. It starts with a
[straight line]{.highlight-cyan} and ends with [machines that can hold the world in
their heads]{.highlight-magenta}: robots, agents, glasses that understand the room you
are standing in.
:::

::: {.quote-card}
The hero of this story was sitting in [chapter two of your statistics
textbook]{.highlight-amber} the whole time. I will not tell you what it is yet. But you
have already seen it tonight, thousands of times.
:::

:::

##  {background-image="assets/content-bg.png" background-size="cover"}

[CHAPTER FIVE · THE FAMOUS CHARACTER ENTERS]{.section-label}
[Ladies and gentlemen: the neural network]{.slide-title}

::: {.spread}

:::: {.columns .fill-space}

::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-cyan}
[Chapter Four's sum]{.card-title}
a · [1]{.highlight-cyan} + b · [x]{.highlight-cyan} + c · [x²]{.highlight-cyan} + ...

[fixed pieces · tuned coefficients]{.small}
:::
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-magenta}
[A network layer]{.card-title}
w₁ · [x₁]{.highlight-magenta} + w₂ · [x₂]{.highlight-magenta} + b, squashed

[learned pieces · squashed so they bend]{.small}
:::
:::

::::

::: {.closing-line}
Look at it. It is chapter three again: a sum of weighted pieces.
:::

:::

##  {background-image="assets/content-bg.png" background-size="cover"}

[CHAPTER SEVEN · THE HERO WAS THERE ALL ALONG]{.section-label}
[The humble Gaussian [(say: GOW-see-an)]{.pronunciation}]{.slide-title}

::: {.spread}

:::: {.columns}

::: {.column width="48%"}

::: {#6f6a1cd4 .cell execution_count=1}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![](keynote_files/figure-revealjs/cell-2-output-1.png){fig-align='center'}
:::
:::


:::

::: {.column width="52%" .body-text}
You have seen this curve. Exam marks. Heights. Measurement errors. The
[bell curve]{.highlight-cyan}.

It is just a soft bump: **strong in the middle, fading at the edges**. Two
numbers describe it, where its centre is and how wide it spreads.
:::

::::

::: {.quote-card}
A Gaussian is the simplest way to say: *"something is roughly here, and a
bit fuzzy around the edges."*
:::

:::

##  {background-image="assets/content-bg.png" background-size="cover"}

[CHAPTER EIGHT · THE TRANSFORMATION]{.section-label}
[Turn the knobs]{.slide-title}

::: {.spread}

:::: {.columns .fill-space}

::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-cyan .fragment}
[+ position]{.card-title}
It lives in 3D space.
:::
:::

::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-magenta .fragment}
[+ orientation]{.card-title}
It stretches and tilts: an oriented ellipsoid.
:::
:::

::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-green .fragment}
[+ colour]{.card-title}
It takes on appearance.
:::
:::

::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-amber .fragment}
[+ opacity]{.card-title}
A soft, translucent blob of stuff.
:::
:::

::::

::: {.closing-line .fragment}
This is called a [splat]{.highlight-cyan}. And no new mathematics happened in the last ninety seconds.
:::

:::

##  {background-image="assets/content-bg.png" background-size="cover"}

[CHAPTER TEN · ALREADY SHIPPING]{.section-label}
[The humble bump, shipping]{.slide-title}

::: {.spread}

:::: {.columns .fill-space}

::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-cyan}
[DJI Terra 5.0]{.card-title}
Native splat reconstruction in the flagship drone-mapping product. Made in Shenzhen.
:::
:::

::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-magenta}
[NVIDIA Isaac Sim 6.0]{.card-title}
Robots trained inside splat scenes. Paper to industrial product in three years.
:::
:::

::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-green}
[RoboSplat · Shanghai AI Lab]{.card-title}
One human demo becomes thousands of training variations, in splats.
:::
:::

::: {.column width="25%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-amber}
[Scaniverse · Hyperscape]{.card-title}
Splat your own room from a phone or headset, today.
:::
:::

::::

::: {.closing-line}
In every one of these, the splat handles [perception]{.highlight-cyan}. A separate system decides.
:::

:::

##  {background-image="assets/content-bg.png" background-size="cover"}

[YOUR TURN · ABOUT 25 MINUTES]{.section-label-gold}
[Teach a machine to perceive this space]{.slide-title}

::: {.spread}

::: {.body-text}
Your group gets an object or a corner of this room. Design, on paper, how
a machine should perceive it, before you are allowed any AI. The TAs have
your worksheets.
:::

:::: {.columns .fill-space}

::: {.column width="33%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-cyan}
[① Think first · ~12 min]{.card-title}
No phones, no AI. Decide what to [measure]{.highlight-cyan}, from where,
what to [store]{.highlight-cyan}, how to [rebuild]{.highlight-cyan} it,
and what makes it hard.
:::
:::

::: {.column width="33%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-magenta}
[② Bring in AI · ~8 min]{.card-title}
When I call it, use an AI tool. Where does it help? Where must
[you]{.highlight-magenta} stay in charge? Mark what AI did against what
you decided.
:::
:::

::: {.column width="33%"}
::: {.panel-card .card-amber}
[③ A few groups share]{.card-title}
One voice, about 30 seconds: your plan, and the one thing AI changed your
mind about.
:::
:::

::::

::: {.closing-line}
Remember the rule: the machine handles [perception]{.highlight-cyan}. You decide.
:::

:::

## Class reference: typography {.smaller}

`.header-label` [FUTURE SCIENCE × HKU SUMMER INSTITUTE]{.header-label}
`.header-subtext` [A muted secondary line under a header block]{.header-subtext}
`.section-label` [CYAN CHAPTER LABEL]{.section-label}
`.section-label-gold` [GOLD VARIANT FOR DARK-IMAGE SLIDES]{.section-label-gold}
`.big-title` [Display title for hero slides]{.big-title}
`.slide-title` [Content-slide title]{.slide-title}
`.subtitle` [A subtitle line under a display title]{.subtitle}
`.pronunciation` — trails a title at small size: [The humble Gaussian [(say: GOW-see-an)]{.pronunciation}]{.slide-title}

## Class reference: inline and body {.smaller}

`.big-quote` [“A standalone spoken line, large and light.”]{.big-quote}

`.body-text` with inline colour spans `.highlight-cyan` `.highlight-magenta` `.highlight-amber` `.accent`:

::: {.body-text}
Body text at reading size, with a [cyan emphasis]{.highlight-cyan}, a [magenta
emphasis]{.highlight-magenta}, an [amber emphasis]{.highlight-amber} and an [accent word]{.accent}.
:::

`.author-name` and `.author-affiliation`:

[Dr. Presenter Name]{.author-name}
[Affiliation line in the muted credit style]{.author-affiliation}

`.closing-line` centres a small final statement:

::: {.closing-line}
A closing line, centred, at footnote weight.
:::

`.narrow-text` pads the right side so prose keeps a narrow column.

Layout components (the theme's marked structural exceptions):
`.title-layout` with `.layout-top` / `.layout-middle` / `.layout-bottom` gives a
title slide its three zones (top header, centred middle, bottom credit);
`.spread` distributes a content slide's blocks evenly over the height
below the title. Both size only their own children.

